Content: _**These are the best places to travel this summer**_








“Horses are the wings of man.” So goes an ancient Kyrgyz proverb.

A visit to Kyrgyzstan is not complete without taking to horseback. Brimming with thousands of years of nomadic history and nat

**********Extracted claims**********

An ancient Kyrgyz proverb states, "Horses are the wings of man."
Horse trekking in Kyrgyzstan involves traveling on horseback for several days through the wilderness from one yurt camp to another.
Horse trekking is described as the backbone of Kyrgyzstan’s culture, economy, and history.
Kyrgyzstan is a historically nomadic country.
Kyrgyzstan has relied on horses for transport and livelihood for thousands of years.
For centuries, horses have supported nomadic families, Silk Road merchants, warriors, and rural farmers in Kyrgyzstan.
Horses continue to play a crucial role in the lives of Kyrgyz people.
Horse meat is a culinary tradition in Kyrgyzstan.
Kok Boru is the national sport of Kyrgyzstan.
Kok Boru is a traditional game played with dead goats, resembling horse polo.
Netflix released a documentary episode on Kok Boru as part of their _Home Game_ series in 2020.
Since gaining independence in 1992 after the fall of the Soviet Union, Kyrgyzstan has viewed horses as synonymous with Kyrgyz statehood.
Horses are still considered the most reliable and efficient mode of transport in many remote areas of Kyrgyzstan.
The horse remains a steadfast cornerstone of modern Kyrgyzstan, despite a rapidly transforming economy and urban centers like Bishkek.
Azamat Zhumashov is the CEO and founder of KAGAN Tours.
Azamat Zhumashov stated that Kyrgyz people are synchronized with and deeply connected to horses.
Azamat Zhumashov stated that horses are the greatest investment for Kyrgyz people.
Kyrgyzstan is dominated by two major mountain systems: Tien Shan and Pamir Alay.
The Tien Shan mountains are located in eastern Kyrgyzstan.
The Pamir Alay mountains are located in southwestern Kyrgyzstan.
The Tien Shan and Pamir Alay mountain systems cover 90 percent of Kyrgyzstan’s land.
The Tien Shan mountains are locally known as the Celestial Mountains.
The Tien Shan mountains are home to some of Kyrgyzstan’s most famous natural wonders.
The Tien Shan is the most popular destination for horse treks in Kyrgyzstan.
The Tien Shan is notable for its diversity in landscape and species.
Multi-day horse treks can be taken to Song-Kol Lake.
Horse treks can head through Jeti-Oguz for red canyon formations and lush, flower-speckled valleys.
The Tien Shan region contains vast, wildlife-filled forests, rushing rivers, and hidden hot springs.
The Pamir Alay Mountain range is a popular destination for horse treks.
The Pamir Alay is rockier than the Tien Shan.
The Pamir Alay is known for its snowcapped peaks peering over dramatically steep valleys.
Many travelers opt to hike on foot in the Pamir Alay region.
Most horse treks in Kyrgyzstan are highly customizable.
Tour companies offer a variety of lengths for horse trek routes.
The Song-Kol Lake trek is the most popular horse trek in the Tien Shan region.
The Song-Kol Lake trek is recommended for beginner riders.
Song-Kol Lake treks typically last 2-5 days.
Song-Kol Lake treks include views of the alpine lake, towering mountain vistas, and lush green forests.
Treks to Ala-Kol Lake and Altyn Arashan Valley depart from Karakol.
Karakol is a hub for outdoor adventure.
Karakol has local coffee shops, Kyrgyz cuisine, and backpacker hostels.
The Ala-Kol Lake and Altyn Arashan Valley trek includes hot springs in Altyn Arashan and the Arashan River.
Jyrgalan was once a prosperous Soviet mining town.
Jyrgalan is now an oasis for natural beauty.
Several horse routes, ranging from 1-4 days, can be taken from Jyrgylan.
Chon-Kemin Nature Park is 135km (84 miles) from Bishkek.
Chon-Kemin Nature Park is Kyrgyzstan’s longest gorge.
Chon-Kemin Nature Park is nestled between two branches of the Tien Shan.
Chon-Kemin Nature Park features a twisting river, numerous grazing animals, and fir trees.
Chon-Kemin Nature Park is a relatively untouched region.
Tours in Chon-Kemin Nature Park start at one day.
Longer treks from Chon-Kemin Nature Park often connect the park with a journey to Issyk-Kol Lake.
Issyk-Kol Lake is the second largest alpine lake in the world.
The Tash-Rabat to Chatyr Kol trek usually takes around five days.
The Tash-Rabat to Chatyr Kol trek follows an ancient Silk Road route in the far east.
The Tash-Rabat to Chatyr Kol trek passes green rolling hills, rocky mountain passes, and the Tash Rabat caravanserai.
The Tash Rabat caravanserai is a 15th-century stone-built building.
The Tash Rabat caravanserai is believed to have served as a rest stop for Silk Road merchants.
A pre-obtained border permit is necessary to visit Chatyr Kol as part of a trek.
Permits for Chatyr Kol can take up to two weeks to obtain.
Tourist companies such as Kyrgyz Tourism and Too.kg offer assistance in obtaining Chatyr Kol permits.
The Tulpar-Kol to Peak Lenin Base Camp excursion takes riders through the Pamir region.
This excursion offers a chance to spend the night at Peak Lenin Base Camp.
Lenin Peak is known as the world’s easiest 7000m (23,000 ft) peak to climb.
Both Ak Tor Pass and Kum Bell Pass Alay treks offer views of the rugged Pamir-Alay region.
These treks offer a chance to immerse oneself in the lives of local families who relocate to the mountains in the summer months.
The Arslanbob Walnut Forest is located outside the Tien Shan and Pamir Alay ranges.
The Arslanbob Walnut Forest is estimated to be 50 million years old.
The Arslanbob Walnut Forest is home to around 130 varieties of nuts and fruit.
The Arslanbob Walnut Forest is the largest forest of its kind in the world.
The Arslanbob Walnut Forest is located 70km (44 miles) from Uzbekistan in the southwest Babash-Ata mountains.
Most people living in the Arslanbob area are ethnic Uzbeks.
Ethnic Uzbeks in the Arslanbob area speak a local dialect, wear traditional clothing, and share a distinct culture.
Arslanbob Village serves as the main hub before heading into the Arslanbob Walnut Forest.
Notable horse excursions in the Arslanbob area include the Sary-Chelek Trek and Toktogul Valley.
Plenty of local and international tour companies organize horse treks in Kyrgyzstan.
Visit Alay is a popular Kyrgyz-based entity.
Visit Alay offers horse treks, hiking treks, and Pamir Highway tours.
Other trusted operators include Gulsary Stables, Datka Travel, and Kyrgyzstan Nomad Trekking.
Community Based Tourism Kyrgyzstan focuses on using a sustainable and holistic ecotourism approach.
Community Based Tourism Kyrgyzstan aims to improve living conditions in remote areas.
Community Based Tourism Kyrgyzstan relies on local resources and manpower.
Travelers can book with Community Based Tourism Kyrgyzstan ahead of time or directly at one of their local offices.
Bulak Say Horseback Riding and Trekking is a tour company.
Most horse treks in Kyrgyzstan leave from Bishkek, Karakol in the east, or Osh in the southwest.
Getting to Karakol from Bishkek requires a 7-8-hour car ride.
There are numerous spots to stop along the way from Bishkek to Karakol for a dip in Issyk-Kol Lake.
Karakol National Airport opened in late 2024.
Flights from Bishkek to Karakol are still few and far between.
Osh is more connected to Bishkek than Karakol.
Several flights a day operate between Osh and Bishkek.
It is possible to fly between Osh and Istanbul.
The southern region of Kyrgyzstan is more internationally accessible via Osh.
Depending on the trekking company, participants in a horse trek might stay in tents or traditional yurt camps.
Yurts are ingrained in the national identity of Kyrgyzstan.
Kyrgyzstan’s flag features a red backdrop with a stylized tunduk in the center.
The tunduk is the top opening of a traditional yurt.
Yurt camps exist across Kyrgyzstan in the summer season.
Yurt camps are located in riverside communities and jailoos (high mountain pastures).
Yurt camps are found in isolated parts of the Kyrgyz landscape.
Camps are collections of several yurts.
One yurt typically serves as the kitchen in a camp.
Other yurts serve as bedrooms or dining areas.
The bathroom situation varies from camp to camp.
Camps are largely family-run.
Children on summer break commonly help out with dinner and play with guests at camps.
At a tent site or yurt camp, meals are typically served banquet style.
Tables are laid out with traditional breads, fruit, cookies, jams, and other snacks.
The main dish will likely be a hearty bowl of rice with meat (beef, mutton, horse or yak) or a soup.
Dietary restrictions can be honored during meals.
Breakfast might consist of freshly made ayran or eggs.
Ayran is a fermented drink similar to yogurt.
Touring companies typically organize abundant picnic lunches during long days of horseback riding.
Kumis is the traditional Kyrgyz alcoholic beverage.
Kumis is made from fermented horse milk.
Horse trekking in Kyrgyzstan is a safe activity for people of all equestrian skill levels.
Tours are led by practiced local horse guides.
Many local horse guides have been riding since nearly before they could walk.
Novices may be placed with calmer horses during treks.
Novices may be led by nose rein for the first few days of a trek.
More advanced participants can gallop freely in some of the fields.
Sam McManus is the managing director of YellowWood Adventures.
YellowWood Adventures is a travel company specializing in Kyrgyz horse tours.
Sam McManus stated that participants are in very capable hands of local nomadic horsemen.
Horse trekking in Kyrgyzstan is only possible in the summer.
Snow tends to pile up deeply in Kyrgyzstan in the colder months.
Late May to early September is the typical timeframe for horse trekking in Kyrgyzstan.
The timeframe for horse trekking shrinks in more remote areas of Kyrgyzstan.
July and August are considered peak season for horse trekking in Kyrgyzstan.
Sturdy hiking boots are a must for a Kyrgyz horse adventure.
Layers, rain gear, and sun protection are recommended for a Kyrgyz horse adventure.
It can get very hot in parts of Kyrgyzstan.
Temperatures can cool down quickly in parts of Kyrgyzstan.

