Loulé Criativo workshops were created to preserve ancestral crafts.
Loulé Criativo studio-cum-museum spaces are designed to showcase handicrafts at work.
Copper is beaten into _cataplana_ cooking pots in the workshops.
Centuries-old palm tree weaving techniques are demonstrated in the workshops.
Moorish-era pottery is spun in the workshops.
Some of Loulé’s museums remain free every opening day.
The Islamic Baths archaeological site in Loulé is free every opening day.
Loulé’s main museum, including part of the castle walls, costs €1.62 (US$1.85) to enter.
Children under 13, adults 65 and older, and students enter Loulé’s main museum for free.
The Algarve’s _passadicos_ (boardwalks) are all free to access.
Boardwalks are encountered crossing dune beaches, craggy cliffs, and in the mountains.
Wooden walkways link Lagos and the Ponta da Piedade headland.
Staircases along the walkways descend to Praia do Camilo and Praia Dona Ana.
A viewing platform overlooks the sandstone sea stacks.
All beaches in the Algarve are free to access.
Renting a parasol and sun loungers from concessions typically costs around €15 (US$17) per day for two.
