Praia de Odeceixe is a river-wrapped beach.
Aljezur has a Moorish castle.
Dinosaur footprints are fossilized on Salema’s beach rocks.
The Pontal da Carrapateira Trail offers a beach-hopping overview.
Southern sections of the Fishermen’s Trail are better connected by public transport.
Buses along the west coast are limited.
Vamus line 47 has multiple daily connections from Lagos to Vila do Bispo.
Section 10 of the trail from Vila do Bispo to Sagres is mostly coastal and 20km (12.5 miles) long.
Faro’s Vila-Adentro (Inner Old Town) is a location for free city walking tours.
Tavira has bougainvillea-adorned backstreets and colorful doors.
Tavira has a cluster of free historical sights.
Free historical sights in Tavira include a crowning Moorish castle, a small and fenced Phoenician excavation site, and a reconstructed Roman bridge.
Beyond the _salinas_, Tavira has a tuna fishing museum.
Tavira is famous for its 30-odd churches.
Larger churches in Tavira charge an entry fee.
A combined ticket for Tavira churches is available.
Smaller chapels in Tavira, such as Ermida de São Sebastião, are free.
Ermida de São Sebastião features a hand-painted ceiling.
Excavations of the 12th-century Almohad Quarter are hidden inside the Convento Da Graça, which is now a hotel.
Convento Da Graça is free to enter.
Waterfalls are free.
Pego do Inferno in Tavira is a cascade-fed swimming hole.
Alte is a picturesque rural hill hamlet.
Alte is hemmed by citrus groves.
Alte is sliced by fontes (natural spring streams).
Queda do Vigário waterfall is located near Alte.
A 13th-century watermill is located near the _fontes_ in Alte.
Casa do Esparto is a grass-weaving workshop in Alte.
Casa do Esparto is part of Loulé Criativo.
Fabrica de Brinquedos is an abandoned school-turned-toy factory near Alte.
Small and free museums and attractions exist in Salir and Penina.
Street art has added color to the Algarve’s whitewashed canvas.
Street art in the Algarve creates outdoor galleries and installations.
Bordalo II is one of Portugal's celebrated street artists.
Bordalo II is known for fusing upcycled trash with environmental messaging.
Bordalo II has larger artworks on Praia de Faro’s water tower.
