Sitting on plastic stools or tarps and sipping strong coffee before sunrise is a common activity in Saigon.
The area around Notre Dame Cathedral and Central Post Office comes to life at night.
Fresh coconuts and sugary soft drinks are sold in the evening around Notre Dame Cathedral and Central Post Office.
Snack vendors selling traditional and trendy Vietnamese snacks frequently pass through the area around Notre Dame Cathedral and Central Post Office at night.
Book Street is located to the side of the Central Post Office and across from Notre Dame Cathedral.
Many of Vietnam’s top publishers have storefronts on Book Street.
Books on Book Street are mostly in Vietnamese.
Some storefronts on Book Street have English-language sections.
English-language titles on Book Street focus on Vietnamese history and culture, translated fiction, and writing-related souvenirs.
The War Remnants Museum is included in most Saigon tourism itineraries.
The American War is little discussed in modern Vietnam.
The War Remnants Museum reveals painful realities of the war, including the My Lai massacre and prison camps on Phu Quoc and Con Dao.
The War Remnants Museum uses photographs and artifacts.
The War Remnants Museum's primary focus is on the activities of America and its allies.
The War Remnants Museum also features material related to the French colonial period and past conflicts with China.
Ho Chi Minh City's Water Taxi service was introduced in 2017.
The Water Taxi service is a practical method of public transportation.
Water Taxi boats depart from Bach Dang Wharf in District 1.
Water Taxi boats meander along the Saigon River.
Notable sights passed by Water Taxi boats include Jade Emperor Pagoda, Skyview, and the surrounding Central Park.
The Water Taxi ride offers glimpses into private yards of Thao Dien’s opulent villas.
The Water Taxi ride offers views of some of Binh Thanh’s humble neighborhoods.
Travelers can stop at five stations along the Water Taxi route.
The Thanh Da neighborhood has a long riverside park and market.
Water Taxi tickets are 15,000d one-way.
Water Taxi tickets often sell out, especially on weekends.
