Vietnamese modernism includes elements specific to Vietnam, considering the tropical climate and local cultural preferences.
Buildings exemplifying Vietnamese modernism include the Reunification Palace, the General Sciences Library, the VAR building, and the central statue at Turtle Lake.
Vietnamese modernist style uses double-skin façades with ornate geometric patterns, overhanging rooftops, and mosaics.
The Vietnamese modernist style includes elements of brutalism.
Locals visit District 2’s Thu Thiem area at sundown to fly kites.
Thu Thiem is a stretch of prime real estate undergoing new developments.
Some kite enthusiasts in Thu Thiem bring their own impressive dragon kites and classic Vietnamese designs.
Simpler kites are sold by local vendors in Thu Thiem.
Street foods such as fried corn, local sausages, sugar cane juice, fresh coconuts, and soft drinks are available in Thu Thiem.
Ho Chi Minh City has an emerging luxury goods scene.
Markets in Ho Chi Minh City sell cheap crafts and souvenirs.
Young designers in Saigon produce clothing designs, prints, art books, and other items.
District 1 offers an introduction to Saigon's fashion scene.
Many small shops are located in the New Playground complex at 26 Ly Tu Trong and an adjacent former apartment complex in District 1.
OhQuao specializes in prints, stickers, and gifts.
OhQuao operates in two locations: one in District 3 and one in Thao Dien ward of District 2.
Thao Dien Ward is considered Ho Chi Minh City's expat hub.
Thao Dien Ward features bakeries, upscale cafes, clothing boutiques, art galleries, and fine dining experiences.
Cafe Slow in Thao Dien offers artisanal coffee sourced from Vietnamese farmers.
Soma in Thao Dien offers vegan-friendly lunch options.
Mami Cocktails in Thao Dien offers cocktails and fusion tapas.
Breadventure in Thao Dien offers pastries.
Ho Chi Minh City has a Japanese community.
The Japan Town off Le Thanh Ton can be overwhelming and seedy.
The Pham Viet Chanh neighborhood in Binh Thanh District is a location for authentic Japanese food and ambiance.
Cozy izakaya and sushi bars in Pham Viet Chanh cater to Japanese expats and Vietnamese people.
Trendy bars such as Birdy, Khaoi, and Lozzi are located in Pham Viet Chanh.
Saigon's Opera House is one of the city’s historic buildings.
Saigon's Opera House is an entertainment and culture venue.
The Opera House was built by the French at the turn of the 20th century.
The Opera House has served various functions over decades, including temporary housing and as the venue for the National Assembly.
The AO Show at the Opera House begins with an introduction to the building’s history and French design.
The Opera House's façade is modeled after Paris’ Petit Palais.
The AO Show combines gymnastics, modern dance, and aerial ballet with cultural mythmaking.
The Ho Chi Minh City Ballet Symphony Orchestra performs at the Opera House.
The Opera House can be chilly due to air conditioning.

