Content: _**These are the best places to travel this summer**_








The gateway to the islands of the Inner Hebrides, the seaside town of Oban sits at the heart of the Scottish Highlands. With trains zipping here from Glasgow and Edinburgh
and ferries connectin

**********Extracted claims**********

Oban is a seaside town located at the heart of the Scottish Highlands.
Oban serves as the gateway to the islands of the Inner Hebrides.
Trains connect Oban to Glasgow and Edinburgh.
Ferries connect Oban to half a dozen islands offshore.
Oban’s history began with whisky.
Oban Distillery was founded in 1794.
The founding of Oban Distillery led to the small fishing village of Oban flourishing into a busy seaside resort.
Victorians arrived in Oban Bay on the *Comet* steamship.
Famous writers and composers, including Robert Louis Stevenson and Felix Mendelssohn, took inspiration from voyages to the wild, sparsely inhabited isles of the Inner Hebrides.
Oban is known as the "Gateway to the Isles."
Oban is referred to as the “Seafood Capital of Scotland.”
Local fishing boats bring their catch to Oban's Railway Pier.
Oban Seafood Hut is located on Oban's Railway Pier.
Oban Seafood Hut offers prawn sandwiches, mussels cooked in white wine and garlic, and seafood platters.
Ee-Usk is a restaurant located on North Pier in Oban.
Ee-Usk serves shellfish from Loch Linnhe, scallops from Mull, and lobster from Luing.
Coast restaurant has been operating for over 20 years.
George Street Fish Restaurant & Chip Shop offers takeaway fish and chips.
Oban Distillery is one of Scotland’s smallest and oldest whisky makers.
Oban Distillery has over two centuries of experience.
Oban Distillery uses traditional methods and only two pot stills to create its single malt whisky.
The Oban Inn was established in the same decade as the Oban Distillery.
The Oban Inn features weekly live-music lineups and traditional music sessions.
The View is a venue across the street from the Oban Inn, offering food and drinks on a waterfront terrace.
The View hosts Ceilidh Nights twice weekly during peak season.
McCaig’s Tower is located on Battery Hill, which is the backdrop to Oban’s main thoroughfare.
McCaig’s Tower was commissioned by local banker John Stuart McCaig in 1897.
The purpose of McCaig’s Tower was to provide work for the town’s stonemasons and to create a memorial for the McCaig family.
McCaig's Tower offers views of Oban and ferries in transit from its viewing platform.
McCaig's Tower includes well-maintained gardens.
Oban Guided Tours offers walking tours exploring Oban’s architectural heritage.
Dunollie Castle is located 1 mile (1.6km) from the center of Oban.
Dunollie Castle is the ancestral seat of Clan MacDougall of Lorne.
Dunollie Castle has guarded the coastline since the 13th century.
Dunollie Castle offers views across Oban Bay to the Inner Hebrides.
Dunollie Castle features exhibits in the 1745 House Museum.
Dunollie Castle grounds contain groves of temperate rainforest.
The Kettle Garden Cafe is located within Dunollie Castle.
The Draper’s Shop is located within Dunollie Castle and sells traditional tartans and textiles.
Dunstaffnage Castle was built as a Clan MacDougall stronghold in the 13th century.
Dunstaffnage Castle was captured by Robert the Bruce during the Wars of Independence in 1309.
Near Dunstaffnage Castle are the ruins of a 13th-century chapel with Campbell tombs.
West Coast Motors buses can be taken to Dunbeg to reach Dunstaffnage Castle.
North and South Ledaig and Benderloch have long beaches with views over Ardmucknish Bay.
Ganavan Sands is a beach near Oban.
Ganavan Sands is accessible by bus from Oban town center.
Ganavan Sands offers views across the bay toward Mull.
The waters at Ganavan Sands are sheltered.
Dougie Dan’s Grill van is parked inland from Ganavan Sands.
The West Coast Motors 417 bus reaches Ganavan Sands from Oban in 10 minutes.
Oban Whisky & Fine Wines sells premium brands, old and rare whiskies, and island gins.
Made in Argyll at Railway Pier features crafts from local artists and makers.
Orsay on Stevenson St is a gallery selling curated gifts from across the country.
The Highland Soap Company on George St specializes in handmade soaps, skincare, and home fragrances using natural and organic ingredients.
The Oban Chocolate Company sells handmade bars, truffles, and boxes of chocolates.
The Sea of the Hebrides and the coastline around Oban are rich in marine life.
Marine life in the area includes seabird colonies, seals, harbor porpoises, minke whales, dolphins, and basking sharks.
Basking sharks are present in the area from June to September.
Oban Sea Tours offer two-hour cruises in the Firth of Lorne, combining history, scenery, and wildlife-spotting.
Sea Kayak Scotland offers guided kayaking adventures around Oban’s coastline.
Basking Shark Scotland offers environmentally responsible tours to swim with plankton-eating basking sharks.
Basking Shark Scotland offers a three-hour guided introduction to snorkeling in the waters around Oban on the Scottish Wildlife Trust’s North Argyll Snorkel Trail.
The Isle of Kerrera is the closest island to Oban.
The Isle of Kerrera can be reached by water taxi from Oban or by foot-passenger-only Caledonian MacBrayne (CalMac) ferry from Gallanach.
Gallanach is located about 2 miles (3.2km) south of Oban.
E-bikes can be hired at the Kerrera Marina on the Isle of Kerrera.
The honesty shop at Balliemore Farm on Kerrera sells cold drinks, island crafts, and ice cream.
The Kerrera Tea Garden serves traditional Scottish stovies, lentil soup, cakes, muffins, and hot drinks.
Gylen Castle is an atmospheric 16th-century ruin on Kerrera.
Gylen Castle was one of a string of castles built by the MacDougall clan.
The ferry terminal at Gallanach can be reached on foot from Oban in about half an hour or by taxi in a few minutes.
Explore Lismore offers bespoke tours of the Isle of Lismore.
The Isle of Lismore is about 5 miles (8km) north of Oban.
Tours of Lismore include exploration by private Land Rover.
Notable stops on Lismore tours include the crumbling 13th-century ruin of Castle Coeffin, the Sailean Lime Quarry, and the Lismore Gaelic Heritage Centre.
Lismore Bike Hire offers bike rentals.
Port Ramsay is a settlement created for limestone workers 200 years ago.
CalMac ferries run daily from Oban to Mull, Coll, Tiree, Iona, and other islands.
The crossing from Oban to Craignure on Mull takes less than an hour.
West Coast Motors buses run from Craignure to Tobermory on Mull.
Tobermory is recognizable by the rainbow facades along its harbor.
The Mull Aquarium is due to reopen after a refurb in 2025.
A forest path from the Mull Aquarium leads into Aros Park.
Glengorm Castle & Estate, Croig Harbour, and Loch Na Keal are good spots for photographers on Mull.
Ben More is 1174m (3852ft) high and is the only Munro in the Hebrides outside of the Isle of Skye.
Ben More can be climbed in six to eight hours from Benmore Farm.
Croig Harbour and Loch Na Keal are haunts for otters.
Eagles are often seen cruising overhead on Mull.
The Rubha nan Gall Lighthouse offers accommodation in its former lighthouse-keeper’s quarters.
Tobermory Distillery is known for its single malts.
The Mishnish pub is located on Tobermory harbor.
The ferry from Fionnphort on Mull’s southwestern tip travels to the island of Iona.
Iona Abbey was built over a monastery founded by St Columba in 563 CE.
White Strand of the Monks and Bay at the Back of the Ocean are beaches on Iona.
