Content: _**These are the best places to travel this summer**_








Driving in Iceland is a dreamy experience, and having your own set of wheels offers the freedom to explore the country at your own pace.

Picture the scene: you’re gliding along a road curling 

**********Extracted claims**********

Having one's own set of wheels in Iceland offers the freedom to explore the country at a personal pace.
The article presents eight selected Iceland road trips.
The Blue Diamond tourist route starts and finishes at Keflavík International Airport.
The Blue Diamond tourist route has an approximate distance of 75–115km (50–70 miles).
The Blue Diamond tourist route loops around the lava landscapes of Reykjanes Peninsula, through seaside villages and the Reykjanes Geopark.
The Reykjanes Geopark was recognized as a UNESCO Global Geopark in 2015.
The borders between the American and Eurasian tectonic plates are visible on the Reykjanes Peninsula.
The Reykjanes Peninsula shows signs of geothermal and volcanic activity.
The milky blue waters of the Blue Lagoon steam into the sky.
Geldingadalir erupted in 2021 and again in August 2022.
Geldingadalir is currently inactive.
Discoveries on the Blue Diamond tourist route include the shore by Reykjanes Lighthouse and Kleifarvatn Lake.
Kleifarvatn Lake is home, according to legend, to a wormlike monster the size of a whale.
Numerous hiking trails are present along the Blue Diamond tourist route.
Road 435 (Nesjavallaleið) starts and finishes in Reykjavík.
Road 435 has an approximate distance of 225km (140 miles).
Road 435 is open in the summer.
Road 435 offers a route that differs from the Golden Circle.
The Golden Circle is Iceland’s most popular day tour.
Road 435 passes through moss-covered lava landscapes and steep hills.
Road 435 offers a view of Þingvallavatn Lake in the distance.
Marked hiking trails are located along Road 435.
Puffs of steam indicate the approach to Nesjavallavirkjum Power Plant and the ION Adventure Hotel along Road 435.
Road 435 continues along lakes, including Úlfljótsvatn which has a church on its banks.
Road 435 passes through a summer house region where city dwellers go for weekend breaks.
Road 435 leads to the main road past the Kerið crater lake.
Kerið crater lake is an obligatory Golden Circle stop.
The route back can include Gullfoss waterfall, Geysir, and Þingvellir National Park.
All other geysers take their name from Geysir.
Laugarvatnshellar features cave houses.
Gljúfrasteinn Laxness Museum in Mosfellsdalur is the former home of Halldór Laxness.
Halldór Laxness was a prominent Icelandic writer.
Halldór Laxness won the Nobel Prize for Literature in 1955.
The Remote East Fjords drive starts and finishes in Egilsstaðir.
The Remote East Fjords drive has an approximate distance of 820km (510 miles).
The village of Vopnafjörður is home to Selárlaug, a swimming pool on the banks of a salmon-filled river.
Selárlaug is popular for fishing.
The mountain pass Hellisheiði eystri (Road 917) is narrow and steep with a gravel road.
The village of Bakkagerði has a hotel, a spa, and a puffin-watching house.
The Bræðslan music festival is held in Bakkagerði in July.
Bakkagerði is a suitable base for hiking.
The trek to Stórurð is a popular hiking route.
Stórurð is a turquoise lake enclosed by massive boulders.
The maritime heritage of the East Fjords region is honored in elegantly renovated historic houses in Eskifjörður and Neskaupstaður.
Randulfssjóhús in Eskifjörður and Beituskúrinn in Neskaupstaður function as restaurants.
Petra’s Stone Collection museum in Stöðvarfjörður showcases the variety of minerals found in the mountains in eastern Iceland.
Reindeer can be observed on the route back to Egilsstaðir.
The Westfjords route by road and ferry starts and finishes in Reykjavík.
The Westfjords route has an approximate distance of 765km (475 miles).
The route to the Westfjords from Reykjavík passes through the rural region of Dalir.
Erpsstaðir dairy farm provides an opportunity to learn about traditional farming life and sample homemade cheeses and treats.
Historical sites near Dalir include Eiríksstaðir and Guðrúnarlaug.
Eiríksstaðir is a living museum about life in the Viking era.
Guðrúnarlaug is a replica of the pool where the heroine of Laxdæla saga bathed.
Reykhólar is the first Westfjordian town on the route.
Reykhólar is home to Sjávarböðin, a spa specializing in seaweed baths.
Seaweed baths are said to have healing properties.
The drive along the peninsula’s southern coast features steep gravel roads.
Rauðasandur is a red-sand beach.
Látrabjarg is Iceland’s westernmost point.
Látrabjarg is a haven for birdlife.
Thousands of seabirds, including puffins, razorbills, and guillemots, nest in the cliffs at Látrabjarg.
A ferry from Brjánslækur travels via Flatey to Stykkishólmur on Snæfellsnes.
Flatey is an idyllic island.
Only seven people live on Flatey year-round.
Flatey used to be the bustling center of the Breiðafjörður islands.
The Library of Water in Stykkishólmur is a modern art museum.
Sjávarpakkhúsið in Stykkishólmur offers locally sourced seafood.
The Snæfellsnes Peninsula route starts and ends in Reykjavík.
The Snæfellsnes Peninsula route has an approximate distance of 440km (275 miles).
The ice cap Snæfellsjökull is visible from Reykjavík in clear weather.
The Snæfellsnes Peninsula features varied landscapes, including sand and pebble beaches, volcanoes, a glacier, rhyolite mountains, lava fields, grassy terrain, caves, bird-filled cliffs, and waterfalls.
The peninsula’s southern side includes the white-sand beach Löngufjörur and the lava landscape around Búðir.
Löngufjörur is a popular location for horseback riding.
Snæfellsjökull National Park is located on the southern side of the peninsula.
The northern side of the peninsula has fishing villages and Kirkjufell mountain.
Kirkjufell is one of Iceland’s most photographed mountains.
Kirkjufell is particularly popular for photography when the Northern Lights are out.
The ancient Kjölur route starts and finishes in Reykjavík.
The ancient Kjölur route has an approximate distance of 1378km (856 miles).
Road 35 splinters off from Route 1 past Gullfoss waterfall.
Road 35 continues along the ancient Kjölur route between Langjökull and Hofsjökull glaciers.
Road 35 is an F-road.
F-roads require a 4WD vehicle.
Road 35 is only open in the summer.
Road 35 does not involve river crossings.
Hveravellir is located around the halfway point of the route.
Hveravellir is an oasis in the desert landscape.
Hveravellir has a lodge, a natural pool for bathing, and a multicolored geothermal area with marked hiking trails.
The route includes trails to Kerlingarfjöll mountains.
The route re-enters the Ring Road about an hour’s drive from Akureyri.
Akureyri is described as the “capital” of the north.
Fáskrúðsfjörður has a museum honoring French sailors.
The museum in Fáskrúðsfjörður is located inside a renovated French hospital.
Microbrewery Beljandi is located in Breiðdalsvík.
Beljandi microbrewery is named after a waterfall.
Djúpivogur features granite egg sculptures in Gleðivík by artist Sigurður Guðmundsson.
The Diamond Circle route starts and finishes in Húsavík.
The Diamond Circle route has an approximate distance of 250km (155 miles).
The Diamond Circle is North Iceland’s equivalent to the Golden Circle.
Ásbyrgi is located at the northern realms of Vatnajökull National Park.
Ásbyrgi is a horseshoe-shaped canyon characterized by vertical cliffs.
Eyjan offers a view of the North Atlantic and sand beaches.
Ásbyrgi is gyrfalcon territory.
Gyrfalcons are the largest falcons in the world.
The Mývatn area features a lake, lava fields, steaming fumaroles, and bubbling hot springs.
Goðafoss waterfall is located in Skjálfandafljót.
Goðafoss is called the “waterfall of the gods”.
Legend states that lawspeaker Þorgeir Ljósvetningagoði threw pagan sculptures into Goðafoss after Icelanders converted to Christianity in 1000 CE.
Aldeyjarfoss is a waterfall surrounded by columnar basalt located upstream.
Húsavík is a whale-watching town.
Geosea Geothermal Sea Baths are located on Húsavíurhöfði next to a yellow lighthouse.
Geosea Geothermal Sea Baths offer an unobstructed view of the whale-watching boats on Skjálfandi Bay.
The Arctic Coast Way starts in Hvammstangi and finishes in Bakkafjörður.
The Arctic Coast Way has an approximate distance of 900km (560 miles).
The Arctic Coast Way is designed for slow travel.
The Arctic Coast Way runs along Iceland’s northern coastline.
The Arctic Coast Way encompasses sleepy villages, six islands, and natural sites.
Hvammstangi is a seal-watching town.
Bakkafjörður is described as windblown.
At Þrístapar, one can learn about Agnes Magnúsdóttir.
Agnes Magnúsdóttir is the heroine of Hannah Kent’s Burial Rites.
Agnes Magnúsdóttir was the last woman executed in Iceland.
Brimslóð in Blönduós offers an option to forage for dinner.
The Museum of Prophecies is located in Skagaströnd.
In summer, the midnight sun reflects off the ocean surface.
In winter, the Northern Lights are visible in the night sky, far from light pollution.
The Arctic Coast Way includes stops such as the Hvítserkur sea stack, the Arctic Henge in Raufarhöfn, and the gannet colony at Stóri Karl on Langanes.
The Arctic Henge in Raufarhöfn is an ancient stone monument inspired by Norse mythology.

