Content: _**These are the best places to travel this summer**_








_Chawadee Nualkhair_ _is a food writer specializing in street food and has written food-focused guidebooks spanning the length of Thailand, including the third edition of "Thailand's Best Stree

**********Extracted claims**********

Each shopping experience in Thailand offers a unique insight into Thai culture and cuisine.
Local wet and floating markets in Thailand are often microcosms of the neighborhoods they serve.
Markets provide intimate glimpses into other lives and tables.
Markets offer informal tours that can vary in expense and length.
Thailand's many night markets are places for both tourists and locals.
Night markets offer cheap food, gimmicky snacks, and bucket cocktails.
Gimmicky snacks at night markets include "dancing shrimp," which are baby shrimp tossed in a spicy dressing.
Floating markets offer a more varied food experience than night markets.
Floating markets offer items such as fruit and ready-to-go snacks like mango sticky rice.
Floating markets are considered more authentic than night markets.
Open-air markets are the most popular places for Thais to buy groceries.
Open-air markets are sometimes referred to as "wet markets."
The term "wet market" is used because of the perishable goods and smaller livestock that comprise most of their inventory.
Open-air markets are found in every Thai neighborhood throughout the kingdom.
Each open-air market caters exclusively to its own clientele.
There are subtle and sometimes not-so-subtle differences between Thai open-air markets.
Klong Toei is a nearly 24-hour market located in central Bangkok.
Klong Toei serves as a central hub for Bangkok's food scene.
Klong Toei offers a variety of products, including curry pastes, fresh vegetables, flapping fish, and live frogs.
Or Tor Kor market caters to Bangkok's affluent residents seeking high-end items.
Fruit is individually wrapped at Or Tor Kor market.
Nang Loeng Market sells handmade traditional sweets.
Nang Loeng Market offers old-fashioned snacks.
Nang Loeng Market sells impeccable ready-made curries for consumption on-site or for take-home.
Many Thai locals prefer large supermarkets for a less chaotic experience.
Large supermarkets are known as "supers" in Thai.
Central Food Hall is an upscale supermarket option.
Gourmet Market is suitable for Japanese and Korean products.
Villa Market is suitable for American goods.
Tops and Foodland are mid-range supermarket choices.
Foodland is open 24 hours.
Big C and Makro are budget-friendly supermarket options.
Makro requires membership for shopping.
UFM Fuji Super is Thailand’s only supermarket entirely dedicated to Japanese products.
UFM Fuji Super sells Japanese products, including natto (fermented soybeans).
Convenience stores are ubiquitous throughout Thailand.
7-Eleven operates over 14,000 outlets across Thailand.
7-Eleven offers essentials such as milk, bread, and water.
7-Eleven provides quick snacks including hard-boiled eggs, instant noodles, and toasted ham-and-cheese sandwiches.
Bangkok's diverse neighborhoods offer international flavors.
In areas with large Japanese expat communities, stores like Lawson and Family Mart offer Japanese favorites.
Japanese favorites available at Lawson and Family Mart include onigiri (rice balls) and oden (hot pot).
Markets in Chinatown sell items like shark’s fin, bird’s nest, and ingredients for traditional Chinese medicine.
Shops on Soi Arab (Sukhumvit 3/1) offer oud and shisha pipes.
Little India (Pahurat) features spices like Indian cardamom and curry leaves.
Little India (Pahurat) also offers sweets like gulab jamun and samosas.
Products in Thai wet markets are highly seasonal and local.
Mangoes, mangosteens, and durian are at their peak during Thailand's summer, specifically March and April.
The rainy season in Thailand, from May to October, brings cheap limes, milder chilies, and an abundance of bamboo shoots.
The cool season in Thailand, from November to February, features an abundance of citrus fruits and fresh herbs like coriander and dill.
Thai regional markets have their own specialties.
In Thailand's North and Northeast, the rainy season produces hed proh (false earthstar mushrooms).
Hed proh are highly prized for their explosive flavor.
Ant larvae and glossy ants are found in Thailand's North and Northeast during the rainy season.
Ant larvae and glossy ants are described as tasting like coconut milk.
Freshly harvested rice is available nationwide during Thailand's cool season.
Strawberries, figs, and peaches can be found in Thailand's far North.
Thailand's far South has a Muslim-Thai majority.
In Thailand's far South, dishes like khao yum (herbal Thai rice salad) and turmeric-brushed grilled fish are abundant.
Most markets in Thailand feature a collection of vendors offering simple noodles, fried snacks, or sweets.
These market vendors are usually within walking distance of supermarkets.
Makrut (or kaffir) lime leaves, lemongrass, and galangal are foundational to many Thai dishes.
Makrut lime leaves, lemongrass, and galangal are much cheaper in Thailand than in local markets abroad.
Makrut lime leaves, lemongrass, and galangal freeze well.
Wild grachai (wild ginger) is suitable for seafood.
Prik Thai aun (green peppercorns) are ideal for jungle curry.
Fresh turmeric is used for sour curry.
These herbs and spices are available at wet markets.
These herbs and spices are available at higher prices in supermarkets.
Thai curry paste simplifies making Thai dishes at home.
Brands like Lobo offer a wide selection of Thai curry pastes.
Lobo curry pastes are widely available throughout Thailand.
Buying nam pla (fish sauce) in Thailand is significantly cheaper than at specialty stores abroad.
A wider variety of nam pla brands are available in Thailand.
Bo.lan Grocer, operated by the restaurant Bo.lan, sells artisanal Thai ingredients.
Bo.lan Grocer offers fish sauce from Samut Songkhram and Rayong.
Supermarkets stock nam pla brands such as Megachef and Squid.
Thailand offers both see ew khao (white soy sauce) and see ew dum (thick, dark soy sauce).
See ew dum (thick, dark soy sauce) is ideal for sweet-and-sour stir-fries.
Dek Somboo is arguably the most widely used soy sauce brand in Thailand.
Nam prik pao (roasted chilli jam) is a versatile, umami-rich, and slightly sweet condiment.
Nam prik pao is usually made with shrimp.
Nam prik pao can be used on pasta, in stir-fries, and mixed with cream cheese on a bagel.
Nam makham piek (tamarind juice) is a staple in Thai cooking.
Nam makham piek provides a sweet acidity that is less harsh than lime juice.
Nam tan peep (palm sugar) is prized for its mellower and more rounded sweetness compared to granulated sugar.
Nam tan peep figures largely in traditional pad Thai.
The palm sugar from the central Thai town of Phetchaburi is considered by many to be the best in the country.
Nam dok mai mangoes are available year-round in Thailand.
The peak season for nam dok mai mangoes is March to April.
Mangosteens, rambutans, and lychees are available in Thailand.
Mangosteens, rambutans, and lychees are fresher and cheaper in Thailand than abroad.
Maesri offers tinned curry pastes.
Roza provides Thai pantry staples such as mackerel in tomato sauce.
Canned fruits like rambutan, lychee, and toddy palm fruit are available.
A variety of unique food items are available outside Bangkok.
In Thailand's North, markets like Talad Warorot in Chiang Mai sell buffalo rinds and pork rinds.
Talad Warorot market sells chili paste dips like nam prik num and nam prik ong.
Makhwan, a Sichuan peppercorn-like spice with numbing qualities, can be found in Thailand's North.
In Thailand's Northeast, cow placenta, crickets, and coconut grubs are available.
Jars of sato (rice wine) are available in Thailand's Northeast.
Durian is prominent in Chantaburi, located in Thailand's East.
Durian is a spiky fruit with a strong smell.
Airlines are unlikely to allow durian onboard flights.
Tourists from across Asia visit Chantaburi during the hot season to sample durians.
The original Sriracha sauce, such as Sriraja Panich, can be purchased in Si Racha.
Sriraja Panich is more complex than the Sriracha version popular in the West.
In Thailand's South, kapi (shrimp paste) is valued for its superior flavor.
Coconut-based products, including bowls and utensils, are available in Thailand's South.
Dozens of unique potato chip flavors exist in Thailand that are not found elsewhere.
Unique potato chip flavors in Thailand include miang kham, Northeastern Thai larb, and Thai barbecue.
Taro brand offers spicy fish strips.
Mama brand produces instant noodles, including a shrimp tom yum flavor.
Doi Tung offers flavored macadamia nuts, including a wasabi flavor.
Fresh Thai sweets do not travel well.
Mango sticky rice is available at Suvarnabhumi International Airport.
Packets of dried fruits like mango and mangosteen are easy to pack.
Dried fruits are widely available in Thailand.
Thailand does not have a strong tea culture.
Many traditional tisanes are made from medicinal ingredients in Thailand.
Traditional tisanes include butterfly pea, used for controlling blood sugar.
Traditional tisanes include roselle, used for blood pressure.
Traditional tisanes include bael fruit, used for digestion.
Granite mortar and pestle sets are superior for making pastes.
Granite mortar and pestle sets are heavy to transport.
Wooden mortar and pestle sets are ideal for making salads like som tum.
Haggling is generally acceptable in open-air markets and some specialty shops in Thailand.
Prices in supermarkets are fixed as marked.
Aggressive haggling, involving raised voices or excessive gesticulation, is considered taboo in Thailand.
Vendors selling similar ingredients, such as vegetables and meat, are typically grouped together in wet markets.
In some supermarkets, shoppers must weigh their own fruit and meat and apply the price sticker.
Cashiers in these supermarkets cannot weigh items for shoppers.
Higher-end supermarkets like Villa and Gourmet Market have staff available at weighing stations.
At places like Big C, shoppers may need to actively seek out an employee for assistance with weighing.
Weekends are the busiest shopping times for Thais.
Weekday hours from 10 am to 3 pm are less crowded for shopping.
Stores are able to sell alcohol from 11 am to 2 pm and from 5 pm to midnight.
There is an increase in shoppers when stores begin selling alcohol.
Alcohol sales are prohibited on Buddhist holidays and election days in Thailand.
Hotels are exempt from the alcohol sales prohibition on Buddhist holidays and election days.
Cash is the primary payment method in open-air markets and small neighborhood shops.
In most cases, direct money transfers to vendor accounts are possible with a Thai bank account.
Supermarkets and convenience stores accept credit and debit cards.
Mom-and-pop convenience stores, often located near wet markets, may not accept credit or debit cards.

