Title: top-things-to-do-in-havana
URL: https://www.lonelyplanet.com/articles/top-things-to-do-in-havana
Content: _**These are the best places to travel this summer**_








Havana seduces you slowly, getting under your skin with its street energy, historical heirlooms and charismatic people. It's a complex city with many different layers.

The range of attractions

**********Extracted claims**********

Many attractions in Havana are free to visit.
Habana Vieja is a meticulously renovated UNESCO World Heritage Site.
The Museo de la Revolucion, Capitolio Nacional, Museo de la Ciudad, Museo Nacional de Bellas Artes, and Gran Teatro de la Habana Alicia Alonso are sites related to Cuba's history.
An art factory in Havana allows visitors to listen to internationally recognized musicians.
Havana has nearby beaches characterized by white sand and palm trees.
Habana Vieja was named one of 25 World Heritage sites with best practices in conservation.
City historian Eusebio Leal transformed Habana Vieja into a significant restoration project in the Americas starting in the 1960s.
City authorities have reinvested profits from Habana Vieja into social projects for its community of 100,000 people.
A free walking tour of the historic quarter of Havana departs daily from the Plazuela de Santo Ángel.
The Malecón is an oceanfront sea wall in Havana that stretches for 7km (4.3 miles).
The Malecón extends from the jaws of the harbor to the mouth of the Río Almendares.
The Malecón is a place where people in Havana gather in the evenings.
The Malecón is flanked by crashing waves on one side and aging buildings on the other.
Fishers, musicians, and couples are found on the Malecón.
Fusterlandia is an art district in Havana.
Fusterlandia was developed over two decades by Havana-based artist José Fuster.
In the 1990s, José Fuster began decorating the walls and houses of Jaimanitas, his neighborhood, with murals and mosaics.
The murals and mosaics in Jaimanitas pay tribute to the work of Gaudí and Picasso.
José Fuster expanded the project in Jaimanitas to cover more than 80 buildings, including bus shelters and a doctor's office.
The Fusterlandia project incorporates Cuban motifs and avant-garde sculptures.
Fusterlandia has transformed the fishing neighborhood of Jaimanitas into one of Havana's top sights.
Games of soccer, bici-taxis, and lively conversations are common on Havana's streets.
Activity is found on the Malecón and in Centro Habana west of the Prado.
Habana Vieja possesses a street spirit, especially away from Calle Obispo.
Calle Obispo is Habana Vieja's main tourist artery.
Necrópolis Cristóbal Colón is a large cemetery in Havana.
