Content: _**These are the best places to travel this summer**_








India overwhelms your senses in the best possible way – nowhere else delivers quite the same barrage of sights, sounds and sensations as this continent-sized country at the heart of Asia.

It w

**********Extracted claims**********

India is a continent-sized country.
India is located at the heart of Asia.
India has 1.4 billion inhabitants.
India features snow-capped peaks, velvety beaches, historic temples, and luxuriant palaces.
India has a reputation for being hot and humid.
India's climate is diverse.
India's diverse climate is due to having beaches, mountains, hills, coastlines, and plains within a relatively small geographical area.
The southwest monsoon brings rainy weather to most of India from June to September.
June to September is the ideal time of year to visit the high-altitude deserts of Ladakh.
There is a risk of encountering landslides and floods in Ladakh during the monsoon season.
The far south of India experiences a milder rainy season from October to December.
The ideal weather window for travel in India is from October to May.
Temperatures and humidity rise significantly from March onward, leading up to the monsoon.
The Himalayan foothills offer milder temperatures and good trekking conditions in the spring.
There is no official requirement for vaccinations to enter India.
A yellow fever vaccination is required for entry into India if a traveler is coming from a country where the disease is endemic.
It is important to contact a health professional at least eight weeks before travel to India to ensure vaccinations are up to date.
Vaccinations for diphtheria, tetanus, hepatitis A and B, polio, and typhoid are usually recommended for travel to India.
Typical childhood vaccinations for measles, mumps, rubella, and varicella should be up to date before traveling to India.
Vaccinations for Japanese B encephalitis, meningitis, and rabies are worth considering for longer trips to India.
Monkeys, dogs, and cats can carry the rabies parasite.
Rabies infection is fatal if untreated.
Taking a course of anti-malarial tablets may be advisable depending on the specific travel destination within India.
Northeastern and eastern parts of India, along with the city of Mangalore, have a higher malaria risk.
Taking precautions against mosquito bites also helps prevent dengue fever.
Dengue fever is a viral infection transmitted by mosquitoes to humans.
Sleeping under a mosquito net is advisable to prevent mosquito bites.
Wearing long sleeves and trousers in light colors is advisable to prevent mosquito bites.
Using a repellent and/or a plug-in mosquito killer with a high concentration of DEET (diethyltoluamide) is advisable to prevent mosquito bites.
Travel insurance is essential for travel to India.
Public hospitals in some areas of India are poorly equipped.
Most private clinics and hospitals in India require payment before treatment.
Travel insurance should cover emergency evacuation and any planned adventure activities.
In India, the national emergency numbers are 100 and 112.
A police report, known as a FIR (First Information Report), is required to make a claim on travel insurance after being a victim of crime in India.
Having a local SIM card in India simplifies many activities, including train bookings and online food orders.
An unlocked phone from home or a locally acquired phone is suitable for use with a local SIM card in India.
A local pay-as-you-go SIM package can be obtained from a phone shop upon arrival in India.
Passport photos and photocopies of passport ID pages are needed to complete the application for a local SIM card in India.
India can be very busy from November to February.
Affordable accommodations are typically fully booked during India's peak season (November to February).
Booking accommodations ahead of time is recommended for travel to India.
Train tickets should be booked in advance, especially for popular routes in India.
Train tickets in India can be booked via the government booking site IRCTC, or through local booking sites such as 12Go or Cleartrip.
India officially adheres to the Gregorian calendar.
Major festivals for Hinduism, Buddhism, Jainism, Islam, and several other religions in India follow lunar calendars.
Festival dates in India vary from year to year.
Checking festival dates before booking a trip to India is always advisable.
The Indian government maintains a useful online list of public holidays.
English serves as the lingua franca in most metropolitan areas in India.
Polite hellos, goodbyes, and thank yous are understood in smaller towns in India.
In northern India, "namaste" can be used as a greeting with hands joined in a prayer-like gesture in front of the chest.
"Namaste" means "I bow to you."
When meeting Muslims in north India, "salaam alaikum" can be used as a greeting.
"Salaam alaikum" means "peace be with you."
The correct response to "salaam alaikum" is "alaikum salaam."
The effort to use local greetings is often welcomed more than perfect pronunciation.
Shaking hands is a standard business greeting between men in India.
Outside metropolitan regions in India, men and women rarely shake hands.
Only the right hand should be used for shaking hands or passing items, including money.
If invited to someone's home in India, bringing a small gift, such as flowers or sweets, is a safe option.
Shoes should be removed before entering someone's home in India.
It is polite to consume what is offered when invited to someone's home, even if one does not particularly fancy it.
Many religions in India have their own dietary rules.
Muslims in India avoid pork.
Many Hindus in India avoid beef.
Some Hindus and Buddhists in India are vegetarian or vegan.
Many Jains are vegetarians who avoid certain vegetables, including onions, garlic, and potatoes.
Many Jains try to avoid causing harm to all living creatures.
Vegan and vegetarian food is often easy to find in India.
Eating with hands is customary in many Indian restaurants, particularly in parts of southern India.
The right hand should be used when eating with hands.
Rice and curry can be mixed into balls with fingers and brought to the mouth with the thumb.
Some thalis (plate meals consisting of multiple dishes) are served on a washed and flash-heated banana leaf instead of a plate.
Haggling for a fair price is common practice in India when purchasing items from street stalls and open-air markets.
Losing one's temper during haggling is considered extremely bad form.
If a mutually agreeable price cannot be reached, one should politely decline and seek another vendor.
Vendors typically quote a price that is higher than the item's worth during haggling.
The buyer should make a counter-offer and negotiate upwards until a mutually agreeable figure is reached.
The "walking away" tactic may lead to last-minute price adjustments during haggling.
A threshold will eventually be reached below which the vendor will not go.
Including extra items in a purchase may lead to a discount on the overall cost.
Many travelers prefer not to haggle in places where the money directly benefits artisans.
It is advisable to be aware of the rules and expectations when visiting temples, mosques, monasteries, gurdwaras (Sikh shrines), synagogues, and churches in India.
One should always check if entry is permitted to a religious site.
Some temples and mosques are closed to individuals who do not follow the faith.
Mosques may be closed to visitors during prayers or on Fridays.
Shoes should be removed before entering any religious building if requested.
One should be prepared to cover their head with a scarf or shawl in religious buildings.
Generally, legs and arms should always be covered when visiting religious sites.
Some temples in India ban leather goods.
Many religious sites in India do not allow photography.
The soles of one's feet should not be pointed towards a person or deity in India.
Pointing the soles of one's feet towards a person or deity is considered disrespectful.
Touching any person or effigy on the head is considered disrespectful.
It is customary to walk around Buddhist and Hindu shrines in a clockwise direction.
The clockwise ceremonial circuit around shrines is known as a parikrama.
